secondaryOutcomes.description:
Sustained recovery was defined as recovery maintained for at least 14 or 28 days (two analyses), or through end of study, whichever comes first. A patient was considered to have recovered once he or she met the following criteria:

1. is afebrile (\<38.0°C core temperature/37.5°C oral temperature) for at least 48 hours without use of antipyretics;
2. all symptoms have resolved or returned to pre-illness levels (e.g., if patient had respiratory compromise prior to the onset of COVID), except for:

   1. fatigue, anosmia, ageusia or dysgeusia, which may be persistent at level similar to that during the acute illness, i.e., the same level per symptom questionnaire;
   2. chest pain, cough or dyspnea which if persistent must be at least one grade lower than at the start of treatment and no worse than grade 1 (mild).

